## 4. Register epilogues and selector checks

The phased MLP initially used separate launches for h, g and y. I let the partition pass keep the same-element prefix together, so each output thread evaluates g from the just-computed h register. Attention's row reduction prevents this particular prefix fusion. Its middle softmax phases still share one row kernel.

The crossover run found a 32-block 1024×64×1024 product took 0.1355 ms tiled and about 0.094 ms scalar. A trial with more blocks and fewer output rows per block was slower still. I moved the selector from an output-element minimum to output tile coverage on the GB10's 48 SMs. The partition needs a sufficiently large tiled phase to pay for its extra launches, and each phase can independently take its scalar path. The launch also checks available GPU bytes before allocating extra cross-phase buffers and scratch.

Initial inspection with "'`tmux -V` reported 3.4. For subsequent multi-job probes the shell kept the tmux session alive with `wait-for`, and the phase sampler produced three timings per contraction per call. The nvidia-smi sampler saw 2411 MHz for all six tiled 4096³ observations and 2398–2411 MHz in the scalar sample. [Raw clock and phase samples](../scripts/08-phase-timings.jsonl)
EOF
